PASS [phone] tour step 1 caption - This is the lunar south pole, from about 80°S to 90°S, built from NASA LOLA elevation data. ASTERIA has 8 south-pole sites with computed terrain horizons. Of NASA's 9 Artemis III candidate regions (Oct 2024), 3 have a plotted point inside them; the others are listed by name only, because their boundaries are published only as images. Why the pole? NASA notes that its permanently shadowed areas can preserve resources,
PASS [phone] tour step 2 caption - Peak near Shackleton - best-lit point (derived): the Sun skims around the horizon, its centre between -0.1° and +2.6° over these 30 days. The brown skyline is the terrain from LOLA elevation data (-3.0° to +3.7°) and it decides when the Sun is visible: Sun up 66% of the time, against 85% for a perfectly smooth Moon. By contrast, at Apollo 11 near the equator the Sun climbs to 88° and is up 49% of the time in one long
PASS [phone] tour step 3 caption - Earth link: seen from the pole, Earth stays near the horizon and moves up and down a little as the Moon's orientation toward Earth changes (libration), so a direct line of sight comes and goes. In these 30 days Earth's centre moves between -7.6° and +5.6° elevation and is in view 10% of the time in 1 window(s); the longest blackout lasts 19.1 days. Earth is hidden now; the next Earthrise is in 7d 23h 08m (2026-12-20 
PASS [phone] tour step 4 caption - Launch slips: each column is a landing date, each row a metric for a 14-day mission. Landing on the nominal date: Sun up 70%, Earth link 21%, both together 13%, longest darkness 4.3 days. A 2-week slip changes that to Sun up 57%, Earth link 0%, both 0%, longest darkness 6.0 days. The best landing in the next 60 days is +52 days with 24%. Click any square to open that date.
PASS [phone] tour step 5 caption - Power check for the nominal 14-day mission: a 1 m², 30%-efficient vertical Sun-tracking panel (example values) generates 98.5 kWh against 33.6 kWh for a 100 W load, so the mission is energy positive. Surviving the longest darkness (4.3 days) needs about 12.8 kWh of battery at 80% usable capacity (example values). First-order estimate only.
